"From Table {#}, compute the sample mean \\(\\bar{x} = \\sum x f / n\\) and the sample standard deviation s (add columns for x·f and (x − x̄)²·f if it helps). Check them against Average x̄ and Sample SD s from part A. Compare them with μ and σ of B({#}, {#}) from Before the lab {#}.",
"Using the P(x) column of Table {#}, find P(x = {#}), P({#} < x < {#}) and P(x ≥ {#}), and the matching relative frequencies from your {#} trials. Which theoretical probability does your sample match worst, and why is P(x ≥ {#}) hard to check with only {#} trials?",
"From Table {#}, compute x̄ and s the same way. Compute μ and σ of the hypergeometric distribution with N = {#}, K = {#} and n = {#}. Compare the two spreads, σ with and without replacement, and your two values of s. Was your prediction in Before the lab {#} right?",
"From Table {#}, compute the average profit \\(\\bar{y} = \\sum y f / n\\) and the expected profit \\(\\mu_Y = \\sum y\\,P(x)\\). Find the theoretical P(x = {#}), P({#} < x < {#}) and P(x ≥ {#}), and the matching relative frequencies of your {#} games. Does your sample of games show a profit or a loss on average?",
"In Table {#}, compute the standard error \\(\\sqrt{p(1 - p)/n_A}\\) of each relative frequency of B given A, where p is P(B given A) and \\(n_A\\) is the number of trials in which A happened (about a quarter of {#}; the contingency table’s A row total gives it exactly). Is each relative frequency within two standard errors of its P(B given A)? Could {#} trials alone have told you that the second card depends on the first when dealing without replacement?",
"Graph Table {#}: the average profit ȳ against the number of trials n. For each row compute the gap \\(\\bar{y} - \\mu_Y\\) and the standard error \\(\\sigma_Y/\\sqrt{n}\\) with \\(\\sigma_Y = 1.1132\\) dollars (the demo’s σ_Y). Is each gap within two standard errors?",
"The trials are simulated from exact probabilities, so the only uncertainty is the scatter of a finite sample. A relative frequency from n trials has standard error \\(\\sqrt{p(1 - p)/n}\\) and an average has standard error \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}\\). About {#} % of samples land within two standard errors (a normal approximation, good when n is large); the shaded band in the Long run graph is exactly this two standard error band. Use it to judge every comparison in the Analysis: a difference smaller than two standard errors is what chance alone gives.",
"Look back at your predictions in Before the lab {#}. Which were right? Explain in a sentence or two why dealing without replacement leaves the mean at {#} but makes the spread smaller.",
